<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Falklands War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1982">1982</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Falklands War was a ten-week conflict in 1982 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and the United Kingdom over the Falkland Islands and South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Argentina's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> occupied the islands on 2 April, and a British task force retook them, forcing an Argentine surrender at Stanley on 14 June. About 650 Argentine and 255 British servicemen died, and the defeat hastened the fall of Argentina's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war was a major episode in a long dispute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> claims the islands as its own territory and described its action as the reclamation of that territory, while the British government regarded it as an invasion of a territory that had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> since 1841. The Falkland Islanders, who have lived on the islands since the early 19th century, are mostly descendants of British settlers and strongly favoured British sovereignty. In 1965 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> called on both states to settle the dispute, and talks made little progress, since the islanders refused to consider Argentine sovereignty. Argentina in 1981 was in economic stagnation and widespread unrest against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> that had governed since 1976.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 2 April 1982 Argentine forces made amphibious landings, known as Operation Rosario, on the Falkland Islands. The garrison of 68 marines and 11 naval hydrographers, with 23 volunteers, offered a brief but fierce defence organised by the Governor, Sir Rex Hunt. South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> was invaded the next day. On 5 April the British government sent a naval task force, eventually of 127 ships, and by early May the Argentine garrison numbered around 13,000. United Nations Security Council Resolution 502 demanded the withdrawal of Argentine troops, and shuttle diplomacy by the United States Secretary of State Alexander Haig failed. Neither state formally declared war.</p><p>British forces retook South Georgia in April. On 2 May a British submarine sank the Argentine cruiser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ara-general-belgrano">ARA General Belgrano</a>, and on 4 May an Exocet missile struck the destroyer HMS Sheffield. British troops landed at San Carlos Water on 21 May, where Argentine air attacks gave the bay the name Bomb Alley. After the battle of Goose Green on 27 and 28 May, in which 961 Argentine personnel were taken prisoner, the British attacked the high ground round Stanley on the night of 11 June, with the hardest fighting at Mount Longdon. A ceasefire came on 14 June, when General Mario Menéndez surrendered to Major General Jeremy Moore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In all, 649 Argentine and 255 British military personnel and three Falkland Islanders were killed in 74 days. Patriotic feeling ran high in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, but the defeat prompted large protests against the ruling military government, hastening its fall and the return of democracy. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the Conservative government was re-elected with an increased majority the following year. Diplomatic relations between the two states were restored in 1989, with no change in either position on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Britain built a permanent military complex at Mount Pleasant, opened in 1985, and the islands remain a self-governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territory</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Falklands War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In eleven years in power she privatised industries, curbed trade unions and fought the <b>Falklands War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
